Fix (attempted): stop preemption from racing an in-flight offload cascade
for the preempted request's own blocks.

Requires patches/preemption-offload-debug-logging.patch to be applied
first (this is an incremental diff on top of it -- same file, same
methods).

Root-cause hypothesis (2026-08-21 conversation, not yet empirically
confirmed by the debug-logging patch's traces at the time this fix was
written -- see that patch's header for full context): a request preempted
mid-prefill goes through _preempt_request() -> _free_request_blocks(),
which -- when the immediate-free branch is taken -- calls
kv_cache_manager.free() synchronously, in the same scheduler pass that
decided the preemption. block_pool.py's free_blocks() only returns a
block to the reusable/cacheable pool once its ref_cnt reaches exactly 0.
An in-flight offload cascade for that block (GPU -> CPU/NVMe secondary
tier) holds its own ref_cnt via prepare_read()/complete_read(),
independent of the existing defer_block_free GPU-step-in-flight guard.
vLLM already has a mechanism meant to drain that cascade before reuse
(jobs_to_flush, populated in offloading/scheduler.py's
build_connector_meta() and waited on in offloading/worker.py's
handle_preemptions()) -- but that flush happens on the worker side, as
part of processing the step's connector metadata, which can happen
*after* the scheduler has already freed (and potentially handed out to
another request) the same blocks in the same pass.

Fix: when a KV connector is active, _preempt_request() now always routes
the freed blocks through the *deferred* free path (force_defer=True),
fenced to the current step, instead of taking the immediate-free
shortcut. Deferred blocks only re-enter the pool once
processed_step_seq confirms that step has actually finished on the
worker side (update_from_output()) -- which is after the worker's
handle_preemptions() wait() on jobs_to_flush has had a chance to
complete, instead of racing it.

Safety-gated on self.defer_block_free also already being True:
deferred-free entries are only ever drained (_drain_deferred_frees(),
called from update_from_output()) when that flag is set. Forcing the
deferred path without it would leak blocks -- queued forever, never
returned to the pool -- instead of fixing anything. This means the fix
is a no-op (falls back to prior behavior) in configs where
defer_block_free is False (single in-flight batch, or a KV connector
that isn't a consumer role) -- verified this repo's actual config has
it True: kv_role="kv_both" satisfies is_kv_consumer, and
--async-scheduling implies max_concurrent_batches > 1.

Round-trip verified (apply/reverse/content check) against vLLM 0.27.1
with patches/preemption-offload-debug-logging.patch already applied.
Not yet verified against a live repro -- that's the next step, using
the debug-logging patch's traces to confirm the fix actually closes the
race rather than just changing its shape.

--- a/v1/core/sched/scheduler.py
+++ b/v1/core/sched/scheduler.py
@@ -1287,7 +1287,17 @@
         assert request.status == RequestStatus.RUNNING, (
             "Only running requests can be preempted"
         )
-        self._free_request_blocks(request)
+        # force_defer=True whenever a KV connector is active: see
+        # _free_request_blocks()'s docstring for why an immediate free here
+        # can race an in-flight offload cascade for this request's blocks.
+        # Gated on self.defer_block_free too -- deferred entries only ever
+        # get drained (update_from_output(), guarded the same way) when
+        # that flag is set; forcing the deferred path without it would leak
+        # blocks (queued forever, never returned to the pool) instead of
+        # fixing anything.
+        self._free_request_blocks(
+            request, force_defer=self.connector is not None and self.defer_block_free
+        )
         self.encoder_cache_manager.free(request)
         self._inflight_prefills.discard(request)
         request.status = RequestStatus.PREEMPTED
@@ -2338,22 +2348,47 @@
     def set_pause_state(self, pause_state: PauseState) -> None:
         self._pause_state = pause_state
 
-    def _free_request_blocks(self, request: Request):
+    def _free_request_blocks(self, request: Request, force_defer: bool = False):
         """Free the request's KV blocks, deferring the return to the block
         pool when an in-flight GPU step may still write them.
+
+        force_defer: always take the deferred path, even if the immediate-
+        free check below would otherwise apply. Used by _preempt_request()
+        when a KV connector is active: a preempted request's blocks may
+        still be mid-transfer in an offload cascade to a secondary tier
+        (CPU/NVMe) at the moment of preemption -- free_blocks() only
+        returns a block to the reusable/cacheable pool once its ref_cnt
+        hits exactly 0, and an in-flight cascade holds its own ref_cnt
+        (prepare_read()/complete_read()) independent of the GPU-step
+        in-flight concern this method otherwise guards against. The
+        connector's own jobs_to_flush mechanism (offloading/scheduler.py,
+        offloading/worker.py's handle_preemptions()) is meant to drain
+        that cascade during this same step's worker-side processing;
+        forcing the deferred path here fences this request's blocks to
+        not re-enter the pool until processed_step_seq confirms that
+        step has actually finished on the worker side, instead of racing
+        it via an immediate free in the same scheduler pass that decided
+        the preemption. See 2026-08-21 conversation: request preempted
+        mid-prefill recomputed from scratch on retry (0% cached, zero
+        kv_offload_load activity) despite the same tier correctly
+        restoring multi-GB of KV cache for requests that had already
+        finished normally before being evicted.
         """
-        immediate = not self.defer_block_free or (
-            # Last scheduled step already processed: no in-flight write remains
-            # (always the case for a normal finish), so free now.
-            request.last_sched_seq <= self.processed_step_seq
+        immediate = not force_defer and (
+            not self.defer_block_free or (
+                # Last scheduled step already processed: no in-flight write
+                # remains (always the case for a normal finish), so free now.
+                request.last_sched_seq <= self.processed_step_seq
+            )
         )
         logger.debug(
             "request-logging preemption-debug: _free_request_blocks(%s) "
-            "status=%s path=%s defer_block_free=%s last_sched_seq=%s "
-            "processed_step_seq=%s num_preemptions=%s",
+            "status=%s path=%s force_defer=%s defer_block_free=%s "
+            "last_sched_seq=%s processed_step_seq=%s num_preemptions=%s",
             request.request_id,
             request.status,
             "immediate" if immediate else "deferred",
+            force_defer,
             self.defer_block_free,
             request.last_sched_seq,
             self.processed_step_seq,
